ukraine and russia are set to hold their  first face-to-face peace talks in more  than two weeks in istanbul joining us is  lord ricketts former permanent  undersecretary at the foreign office and  former british permanent representative  to nato hello to you thanks for joining  us on the programme this morning let's  talk about that poisoning first of all  alleged poisoning that the americans are  pouring a bit of cold water on it but  there is um you know previous obviously  from russia on poisoning people how  concerned should those taking part in  the peace talks be  i think they'll want to be very careful  indeed i mean we don't know what  happened it's quite mysterious  the problem is it could well have  happened because as you say the russians  have a long track record a kind of  morbid fascination with poisons and  chemical weapons so it is entirely  possible that somebody made an effort to  disrupt this informal mediation which is  i think what abramovich others were  trying to do so yeah i'm sure the  ukrainian negotiators in turkey and  indeed the turkish hosts will be being  extremely careful  yeah what's your advice to them people  taking part in peace talks with the  russians  well i i think  depend on your turkish hosts i think  today this is an initiative by president  erdogan i'm sure he will do everything  possible to keep everybody safe at the  venue but it is  a very very depressing conclusion that  you can't trust  russian negotiators and sit down with  them without worrying that there might  be some awful poisoning to be done what  um sort of steps would you take my lord  if you were taking part in peace  negotiations with face to face with the  russian entourage  i think i'd stick with the turkish  supplied  nibbles and water and stay off anything  else  a very good advice um talk to me about  uh president zielinski he uh was talking  i think on sunday night and uh he says  he's now willing to discuss neutral  status in exchange for peace how should  we interpret that  i think everybody wants this terrible  war to be over and we'd all love to  think that negotiations could produce a  breakthrough soon in the ceasefire and  i've absolutely no doubt that president  zielenski and the ukrainian side are  totally sincere but i'm really wary of  the russians here i don't honestly see  that the conditions are there for a  meaningful ceasefire  i think it's brave of president  zielinski to face up to the fact that  ukraine is not going to join nato  anytime soon he's interested in security  guarantees from the major powers and a  neutral status of ukraine possibly with  the right to join the eu  all these are important but they're  frankly secondary issues the key thing  is what happens to this territory that  russian forces have occupied i do not  believe that putin will be willing to  give up places like mario paul once they  do hold it firmly that's on the corridor  between the east which russia already  controls and the south and the crimean  peninsula i'm afraid his minimum  objective is to hold the territory that  he's now occupied  that requires ukraine to accept  effectively the partition of their  country and i just find it very hard to  imagine that any ukrainian government  could do that so i'm pretty skeptical  about the outcome of these talks  although it's a good thing people are  talking yeah even though the alternative  is the pictures that we've just been  looking at where parts of ukraine are  literally being bombed into oblivion  peace talks mean that both sides have to  give  yes they do  but they mean that ukraine would have to  be prepared to give up large slices of  the country  um which would be a kind of reward for  russian  conquest and occupation  i think it's perhaps more likely that at  some point the fighting will diminish  that there'll be some sort of truce and  a uneasy partition across a line of  separation neither side accepting that  is the outcome  that might  be a help for ukrainian citizens and  enable humanitarian help and so on to  them but it will be unstable and it will  guarantee that fighting will flare up  again in the future i don't want to  predict that but i think that's probably  more likely than a signed and sealed  peace deal any time in the foreseeable  future  diplomacy all important in peace talks  especially when you're dealing with a  country like russia how helpful was  president biden's comments at the  weekend weekend when it seemed that he  was suggesting that regime change in  russia was the only option  well i think he'd done extremely well on  this visit to europe he'd had a series  of summits in brussels i think which  he'd played brilliantly i think the  alliance feels stronger as a result  personally i think that remark about  effectively meaning regime change in  moscow was a mistake  because i think we need to keep the  focus on ending this war  and leave the future of president putin  to the russians i think putting russian  president putin in a corner feeling that  the objective of the west is to oust him  will only make things more difficult and  i know that his comments have been um  kind of corrected the sense of perhaps  went going a bit further than he  intended in those remarks  i think the real thing is to get this  war over uh and i think declaring our  objective is to house president putin is  a mistake okay my lord it's always good  to talk to you thank you for sparing the  time we do appreciate it  thank you  thank you
 

